{"id":2965,"date":"2021-06-16T04:32:25","date_gmt":"2021-06-16T11:32:25","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=2965"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:40:32","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:40:32","slug":"loops-1-5","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/loops-1-5\/","title":{"rendered":"1.5: Loops"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de071cf\" name=\"6a2214de071cf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Shapes with Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_single_image image=&#8221;2027&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<p>Usually, certain shapes have a set number of degrees that the internal angles add up to. For example, a square&#8217;s angles add up to 360\u00b0, while a triangle\u2019s angles add up to 180\u00b0. For shapes that have sides of equal length, you will learn how to use a loop to write more efficient code.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de072f9\" name=\"6a2214de072f9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Square code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To appreciate the value of a for loop, first think about the code for driving in a square. Since a square has four equal sides, you would need to go forward then turn left or right four times.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2028&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Use the driving blocks to write a code that drives Zumi in a square. You have done this before so this should be a review![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3228&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de0735a\" name=\"6a2214de0735a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"jupyter-nb-cells-json\"><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">You have probably noticed that you <strong>repeat<\/strong> the same section of code four times. That&#8217;s a lot of blocks! To get around this, you can use a loop, which lets you repeat parts of your code. They&#8217;re a great shortcut &#8212; without them, you would always have to use the same blocks over and over again. They are called loops because the last statement loops around back to the beginning. The loop blocks that help you repeat code are found in the &#8220;Loops&#8221; menu.<br \/>\n<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2968&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de073a4\" name=\"6a2214de073a4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Repeat block<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Any code attached to a repeat block will repeat as many times as you set. This means you can choose how many times your code will repeat! Here is the same square code written with a repeat block.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3237&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de073fd\" name=\"6a2214de073fd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More shapes!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]What other shapes can you make with loops? The square is the easiest because each turn is 90 degrees, but what about other shapes? How many sides does a triangle have? This particular triangle will be an\u00a0<strong>equilateral<\/strong>\u00a0triangle, meaning all three sides are of equal length and every angle is the same number of degrees. Here&#8217;s a hint: the exterior angles of all\u00a0<strong>regular<\/strong>\u00a0polygons, or shapes with equal sides, always add up to 360 degrees.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2030&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]There are many other shapes you can teach Zumi. Try pentagons, hexagons, or octagons! You may need a pencil and paper to figure out how many degrees you need to turn. Remember, the number of degrees multiplied by the number of sides should be equal to 360 degrees.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2031&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de07442\" name=\"6a2214de07442\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More Shapes - Solutions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Once you have tried making the different shapes with Zumi, try comparing your code with the solutions below![\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Pentagon:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3423&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;][vc_column_text]Hexagon:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3424&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;][vc_column_text]Octagon:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3425&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214de07484\" name=\"6a2214de07484\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Review<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned how to use the repeat block! The repeat block is helpful when you want to repeat code a certain number of times. In the next lesson, you will learn about conditionals and how we can make loops run as long as something is true.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Shapes with Loops [vc_single_image image=\"2027\" img_size=\"large\" alignment=\"center\"][vc_column_text] Usually, certain shapes have a set number of degrees that the internal angles add up to. For example, a square's&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/loops-1-5\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2898,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":2878,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2123,2118,2122,2134,2234,2230],"class_list":["post-2965","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-loops","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-shapes","lesson-tag-zumi","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2965","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2965\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12828,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2965\/revisions\/12828"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2898"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=2965"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=2965"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=2965"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}